course: dictionary
lesson: 1426
title: Carnation: a flower and a colour
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives two meanings for carnation. Read the words around it to find out which one is meant. «Put a white carnation in a glass of water with blue ink.» The stem, the water and the petals are clues that carnation means the flower.
explain: Carnation can also be a colour, a pinkish-red. Look for clues like «a red carnation» or words that describe how something looks. Then test your meaning by putting it into the sentence in place of the hard word. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A carnation is a garden flower with frilly petals and a sweet, spicy smell.
fact: Carnations are often pink, red or white.
fact: «Carnation» can also mean a pinkish-red colour, but this meaning is rare.
fact: In a carnation experiment, the white petals turn colour because the stem carries the coloured water up through tubes called xylem.
fact: Carnations are often used in bouquets, as buttonholes at weddings, and on Mother's Day.
exercise: «Put a white carnation in a glass of water with blue ink.» What is a carnation in this sentence? | *A garden flower | A glass of water | A pinkish-red colour | A blue ink pen
exercise: Jo says, «The dress was carnation, a soft pinkish-red.» What does carnation mean here? | A sweet, spicy smell | *A pinkish-red colour | A tall tree | A kind of water
exercise: Mum says, «I bought a bunch of carnations for Mother's Day.» What are carnations often used for? | Lighting a fire | Making blue ink | *Bouquets and gifts | Cooking dinner
test: In the carnation experiment, what happens to the petals? | *They change colour, because coloured water travels up the stem | They turn into a bunch of carnations | They fall off in the water | They grow twice as big in one hour
test: What is the name of the tubes that carry water up a stem? | Petals | Buttonholes | *Xylem | Bouquets
exam: When a word has two meanings, what is the best way to choose the right one? | Look at the first letter only | *Swap each meaning into the sentence and see which one still makes sense | Ask someone who is not reading the sentence | Pick the longer meaning
